ol in required_symbols).\n- Compute all nodes, their depths, etc.\n- Determine removal order: deep to shallow, tie break by id ascending.\n- Maintain set of retained ids, initially all.\n- For each node in order:\n   if node['removable'] and node['id'] != root and node['symbol'] not in required_symbols:\n        # Check no retained children\n        children = [child for child in nodes if child['parent'] == node['id']]\n        if any(child['id'] in retained for child in children):\n            continue\n        # Check if any required symbol in subtree (including descendants)\n        # We need to get all descendants. Could precompute subtree required.\n        # Actually easier: check that no required symbol is in the subtree of this node (excluding itself if not required)\n        # Since we require all required symbols to remain represented after removal, we must ensure that the subtree does not contain any required symbol.\n        # If it does, removal would lose that symbol.\n        # So compute all nodes in subtree (including node). Since node not required, we check descendants.\n        descendants = get_subtree_ids(node['id']) (including node? but node not required)\n        if any(req_id in descendants for req_id in required_ids):\n            continue\n        # Then remove: remove node and its entire subtree from retained? But if we remove node, its descendants become orphaned. In typical reduction, removing a node means deleting that node and its entire subtree. That is consistent with \"never remove a node with retained children\" – we only remove when children already removed, so subtree is empty. So we need to remove children first. So if we have already processed children in deeper order, they would have been removed if they were removable and satisfied conditions. So eventually we remove leaf nodes first, then internal nodes. So we should remove only the node itself (since children already gone). But we also need to update retained set. So we remove node.\n        retained.remove(node['id'])\n\nBut careful: The condition \"never remove a node with retained children\" implies we should not remove a node if any of its children are still retained. That's what we check. So if children are not retained (already removed), we can remove the node. This naturally ensures bottom-up. Also, if a leaf node is removable, it has no children, so condition passes. Then we remove it. Then its parent later, when all children are removed, can be removed if conditions satisfied.\n\nWe also need to check required symbols in subtree. But if we remove children first, they might be required. If a child is required, we cannot remove it. So that child remains. Then when we consider the parent, it still has a retained child (the required one), so we cannot remove the parent because condition \"no retained children\" fails. That prevents removing a branch that contains a required symbol. Good. So the subtree check might be redundant if we enforce bottom-up and children removal only when not required. However, consider a node that is not required but its descendant is required. That descendant will be processed earlier (deeper). If it is required, it cannot be removed. So when the ancestor is processed, it still has that retained child (the required node). So condition \"no retained children\" fails. So we cannot remove ancestor. So we don't need explicit subtree check. But we must also consider that a node might be required itself (not removable). So the condition \"node['symbol'] not in required_symbols\" is enough. Also we must consider that root is required? In fixture, root is in required_symbols. So root cannot be removed. So algorithm: Process nodes in decreasing depth, ascending ID. For each node, if removable, not root, symbol not required, and none of its children are in retained set, then remove it (retained.remove(id)). Also record removal order.\n\nReturn a dict with:\n- retained_ids: sorted list of retained node IDs (maybe